// TASK 2: What is Zero Trust?
$task_contents[2] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Never Trust, Always Verify</h2>

    <p>For decades, network security followed a castle and moat model. Build a strong perimeter, watch the gate closely, and trust anything that gets inside. This worked when employees sat at desks inside one building and every application lived on a server down the hall.</p>

    <p>That world no longer exists. Employees work from home, applications live in the cloud, and attackers routinely gain a foothold using a single stolen password. Once inside a flat, trusting network, an attacker can move from one system to another with little resistance. Security teams call this <strong>lateral movement</strong>, and it is how a single compromised laptop becomes a full breach.</p>

    <p>Zero Trust starts from a different assumption entirely. No user, device, or application is trusted by default, no matter where it sits on the network. Every request is verified explicitly, every time, using signals like identity, device health, and context.</p>

    <div class="info-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> Why This Matters</h4>
        <p>Most modern breaches do not start with a sophisticated exploit against the firewall. They start with a valid set of stolen credentials or a compromised device that the network already trusts. Zero Trust removes that blind trust entirely.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Perimeter Model vs Zero Trust Model</h3>
    <table class="content-table">
