// TASK 3: The NIST SP 800-207 Framework
$task_contents[3] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>A Formal Reference Architecture</h2>

    <p>Zero Trust is a set of principles, not a single product. NIST SP 800-207 gives those principles a formal structure so organizations can design and evaluate real systems against a common reference architecture.</p>

    <p>At the center of the framework sits the <strong>Policy Decision Point</strong>, made up of a Policy Engine that evaluates trust signals and a Policy Administrator that issues the decision. Think of the Policy Decision Point as a bouncer's brain, weighing everything it knows before deciding yes or no.</p>

    <p>That decision then needs to be carried out somewhere close to the actual resource. This is the job of the <strong>Policy Enforcement Point</strong>, which sits directly in front of the application, database, or service and either allows or blocks the connection based on what the Policy Decision Point told it.</p>

    <div class="info-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> Framework, Not a Product</h4>
        <p>NIST SP 800-207 is a reference architecture, not a checklist you can buy off a shelf. Vendors sell tools that implement pieces of it, but Zero Trust itself is achieved through how those pieces are designed and enforced together.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- SVG: Request flow through PEP and PDP
