// TASK 4: Microsegmentation & Software-Defined Perimeter
$task_contents[4] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Shrinking the Blast Radius</h2>

    <p>Even with strong identity checks, a flat network still lets a compromised system reach far more than it should. <strong>Microsegmentation</strong> solves this by dividing the network into small zones, each with explicit rules about what can talk to what. A compromised laptop in one segment simply cannot reach a finance database in another unless a rule explicitly allows it.</p>

    <p>Think of microsegmentation like watertight compartments on a ship. If one compartment floods, the doors around it seal shut and the rest of the ship stays afloat. Without those compartments, a single leak sinks the entire vessel.</p>

    <p><strong>Software-Defined Perimeter</strong> takes this a step further by making resources invisible until a user proves who they are. Instead of a server responding to any connection attempt, it stays completely dark on the network until the requester has been authenticated and authorized, at which point a private connection is created just for them.</p>

    <div class="warning-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-triangle-exclamation"></i> A Common Pitfall</h4>
        <p>Over-segmenting a network without proper planning creates operational chaos. Teams that segment aggressively before mapping how applications actually communicate often break legitimate workflows and spend weeks chasing broken connections.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- SVG: Flat network vs microsegmented network
