// TASK 5: Identity-Aware Proxies & Continuous Verification
$task_contents[5] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Identity as the New Perimeter</h2>

    <p>If identity is the foundation of Zero Trust, something has to enforce it in front of every application, not just at the network edge. This is the role of the <strong>Identity-Aware Proxy</strong>. Rather than one guard at the building's front door, imagine a dedicated guard stationed outside every single room, checking identity before anyone steps inside.</p>

    <p>An Identity-Aware Proxy sits directly in front of an application and evaluates identity, device posture, and context before allowing the connection through. Because every application has its own checkpoint, an attacker who slips past one system still faces a fresh check at the next.</p>

    <p>Traditional access control checks identity once, at login, and trusts the session afterward. Zero Trust replaces this with <strong>continuous verification</strong>, reassessing risk throughout the session so that a change in device health or behavior can trigger a new challenge or revoke access entirely, even mid-session.</p>

    <h3>Traditional Access vs Continuous Verification</h3>
    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th></th>
                <th>Traditional Access</th>
                <th>Continuous Verification</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>When verification happens</td>
                <td>Once, at login</td>
                <td>Continuously, throughout the session</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>If risk changes mid-session</td>
                <td>Session remains trusted regardless</td>
                <td>Access can be challenged or revoked immediately</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Dependency on network location</td>
                <td>High, location often implies trust</td>
                <td>Low, identity and context matter more than location</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="success-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Key Point</h4>
        <p>Continuous verification means trust can be taken away as easily as it was granted. A session that looked safe at login can be cut off the moment new risk signals appear.</p>
    </div>
</div>
HTML;

// TASK 6: Least Privilege & Migration Roadmap
$task_contents[6] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Only What Is Needed, Only When It Is Needed</h2>

    <p><strong>Least privilege</strong> means every user, service, and device receives the minimum access required to do its job, nothing more. A contractor working on one project folder should never be able to reach the finance database, even if convenience would make that access easier to grant.</p>

    <p>Getting an entire organization from a perimeter-based network to a Zero Trust one does not happen overnight. It follows a deliberate order, because skipping steps breaks the steps that come after.</p>

    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Migration Phase</th>
                <th>What Happens</th>
                <th>Why This Order Matters</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>1. Asset inventory</td>
                <td>Catalog every user, device, application, and data flow</td>
                <td>You cannot protect or segment what you have not identified</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>2. Identity foundation</td>
                <td>Establish strong identity and MFA for every account</td>
                <td>Every later control depends on knowing who is asking</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>3. Segmentation and IAPs</td>
                <td>Deploy microsegmentation and Identity-Aware Proxies</td>
                <td>Enforcement only works once identity and assets are known</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>4. Continuous monitoring</td>
                <td>Enable ongoing risk scoring and session evaluation</td>
                <td>Static access rules alone cannot catch changing risk</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>5. Policy refinement</td>
                <td>Tighten rules based on real usage data</td>
                <td>Real traffic patterns reveal where access is still too broad</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="warning-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-triangle-exclamation"></i> A Common Mistake</h4>
        <p>Many organizations buy Identity-Aware Proxy or microsegmentation tools before they have a clear asset inventory or identity foundation. The tools end up enforcing rules against data that is incomplete, which creates gaps instead of closing them.</p>
    </div>
</div>
HTML;

