// TASK 8: Conclusion
$task_contents[8] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <p>You now understand why Zero Trust replaces a single trusted perimeter with continuous, identity-based verification at every point in the network. The NIST SP 800-207 framework gives that idea a formal shape through the Policy Decision Point and Policy Enforcement Point, while microsegmentation, Software-Defined Perimeter, and Identity-Aware Proxies turn it into something you can actually build.</p>

    <p>None of this works without least privilege as the guiding rule and a deliberate migration order that starts with knowing your assets and identities before deploying enforcement. These are the skills that separate a network that merely has a firewall from one that can survive a stolen password or a compromised laptop.</p>

    <h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Zero Trust assumes nothing is trusted by default, inside or outside the network.</li>
        <li>NIST SP 800-207 formalizes Zero Trust through the Policy Decision Point and Policy Enforcement Point.</li>
        <li>Microsegmentation and Software-Defined Perimeter reduce how far an attacker can move after a breach.</li>
        <li>Identity-Aware Proxies and continuous verification make identity the real perimeter.</li>
        <li>Least privilege and a disciplined migration order turn Zero Trust from theory into a working network.</li>
    </ul>
</div>
